# FORK-SITES: issue 84 items 2 and 3 — the exemption list is empty, and closed

`PRE_EXISTING_FORK_SITES` is `frozenset()`. Seven files went through
`local_llm_lab.spawn`, in three shapes, one per reason each had been exempted. `provenance.py`
landed earlier as item 1, because it was the only entry that forked with a model already resident.

## Why an exemption was a real hazard and not bookkeeping

CPython takes `posix_spawn` only when `cwd` is `None` and the program is absolute. Everything else
forks, and a fork in an interpreter that has initialised Metal aborts in libplatform rather than
raising. The abort is `SIGABRT`, so `atexit` never runs, so a held model-run lock is orphaned —
then correctly reported stale and correctly never deleted. One aborted run locks the machine until
a person clears it.

An exemption made a file invisible to **both** enforcers at once: the static rule in
`test_repository_rules.py` and the runtime guard in `conftest.py` read the same set. That is by
design, so the two can never disagree — and it is also why the list had to end up empty rather
than merely short.

## The three shapes

**`git -C`, where a directory was handed to git.** `runlog.git_commit` and
`integrity.git_tree_dirty` passed `cwd=`. The directory moves into argv, where it costs nothing.
Failure behaviour is identical: `git -C` on a path that is not a repository exits non-zero, which
both callers already read as "not determined".

**`/bin/sh -c 'cd … && exec …'`, where the directory is load-bearing.** `chat.py` and
`train_sft.py` start `mlx_lm`, whose YAML resolves `data:` and `adapter_path:` against the working
directory. That cannot be dropped and git's `-C` has no equivalent here, so it moves into argv in
the form `spawn`'s own docstring names: the exec that actually happens stays visible, and `exec`
means no extra process survives. The quoting is not decorative — this repository's root contains a
space.

**Dropping a `cwd` that was doing nothing.** `tests/test_probes.py` passed `cwd=<repo root>` to a
child that imported `local_llm_lab`. The repository root holds `src/`, not the package, so the
child never imported through it; the installed distribution did. The `cwd` bought nothing and cost
the fork path — and this file imports `mlx.core` at module scope, so its fork happened with Metal
already up. Of the seven, this was the live one.

**`check_env.py`** named a bare `sysctl`; the helper resolves it.

## `probes/guard.py` did not migrate. Its check was deleted.

Issue 84 asked for the duplicate concurrency check to go rather than be repaired, and the reasons
were all things that had already happened:

- **It missed real processes.** Detection by `pgrep` against a fixed pattern of job names sees only
  jobs whose command line matches. `ctxmax.py` held 16.6–17.2 GiB for twenty-three minutes on
  2026-09-05 and was invisible to every name check on the machine.
- **It was one entry point from blocking itself.** The pattern excluded only the caller's own pid,
  so the first probe whose command line contained one of those names would refuse to run because it
  found itself. The same self-match cost eight minutes during the hosted-lens conversion.
- **It forked**, and was safe only because it happened to run before the first MLX allocation — a
  property of call order that nothing enforced.

`gpu_users()` is now a call to `runlock.running_model_processes()`, which asks the kernel which
processes have `libmlx.dylib` mapped. `runlock._refusal_for_processes` becomes public so both
callers refuse in one wording; two wordings for one condition is how two definitions of it start.

## What the tests now assert

`tests/test_fork_sites.py` is new and holds one place a reader finds how each site was fixed. The
git helpers are checked twice over: the argv shape, and then a real call against this repository,
because a shape test passes against a helper that builds a correct argv and never runs.

Two existing tests changed meaning rather than being deleted.

`test_the_fork_guard_lets_a_listed_site_through` asserted `runlog.py` **was** in the exemption
list. It is now `test_the_last_exempt_site_no_longer_forks` and asserts it is **not**, making the
same call: the guard raises `ForkInThisInterpreter` on the line that forks, and `git_commit`
swallows `OSError` and `SubprocessError` but not a `RuntimeError`, so a 40-character hash means the
call reached `posix_spawn` and "unknown" would mean git itself failed. Both outcomes stay
distinguishable.

The guard's pass-through branch lost its last user, so it is now driven directly with a stand-in
for the fork primitive. It is **not** tested by exempting a file and calling `os.fork` for real:
pytest imports MLX at collection, so that interpreter has Metal up, and whether the abort fires is
a coin toss on the memory-pool thread's lock. A test that reproduces the crash it documents is not
a test.

## The closure

`test_the_fork_exemption_list_is_empty_and_stays_empty` is stated separately rather than left
implicit in an empty set, because the failure to guard against is not a fork. It is somebody adding
a name to the list to make the static rule go green — the edit that makes a file invisible to both
enforcers, and exactly how the seven accumulated. The list earned its exemptions by predating the
helper. Nothing can earn one now.

## Verification

```
full suite:                     exit 0
ruff, all changed files:        11 findings before, 11 after, 0 newly introduced
                                (all 11 pre-existing in runlog.py, away from the edit)
git apply --check against HEAD: 0
concurrency: no libmlx-mapped process and no model-run lock, checked in the launching command
```

Two notes on scope. Ruff's autofix silently modernised four unrelated things in `runlog.py`
(a `timezone.utc`, a typing import, three quoted annotations); those were reverted and the
file carries only the migration. And `PRE_EXISTING_FORK_SITES` is kept as an empty set rather
than deleted along with its branch, because the issue's acceptance says the list is empty and
the rule refuses additions, not that the mechanism goes. Deleting it is a larger call than
this issue made.

